                  snippet:
                    "Like the previous survey, the unemployment rate is projected to be an annual average of 4.2 percent in 2025 and 4.5 percent in 2026 before falling to 4.4 percent in 2027, and 4.3 percent in 2028. On the employment front, the forecasters predict job gains in the current quarter at a rate of ...\nWilliam Adams, Comerica Bank; Ed Al-Hussainy and Alexander Spitz, Columbia Threadneedle Investments; Scott Anderson and Doug Porter, BMO Capital Markets; Robert J. Barbera, Johns Hopkins University Center for Financial Economics; Peter Bernstein, RCF Economic and Financial Consulting, Inc.; Wayne Best and Michael Brown, Visa, Inc.; Seth Carpenter, Morgan Stanley; Christine Chmura, Ph.D., and Xiaobing Shuai, Ph.D., Chmura Economics & Analytics; Gary Ciminero, CFA, GLC Financial Economics; Grant Collins, AIM Research, LLC; Andrew Davis, Bryn Mawr Trust; Rajeev Dhawan, Georgia State University; B\nFourth Quarter 2025 Survey of Professional Forecasters\nDue to the lack of data from federal statistical agencies, the historical data point for the third quarter of 2025 was unavailable for most variables when we conducted the survey. The forecasters provided a projection for this data point. The only exceptions are for the interest rates and the CPI inflation rates. The survey reports the actual historical values for these variables.\nLike the previous survey, the unemployment rate is projected to be an annual average of 4.2 percent in 2025 and 4.5 percent in 2026 before falling to 4.4 percent in 2027, and 4.3 percent in 2028. On the employment front, the forecasters predict job gains in the current quarter at a rate of 30,800 per month.",
